1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,710
A miter saw kicked, and the
blade caught his forearm.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:08,230
The blood is bright red, and it's
pumping out with every heartbeat, right

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,790
through the gauze under her hand.

4
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:12,777
A bleed like this can
kill in a few minutes.

5
00:00:12,827 --> 00:00:15,480
So what do your hands do, right now?

6
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:20,770
Stick with me, and you'll know which
bleeds you just press on, which ones

7
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:24,850
get a tourniquet right away, where it
goes and how tight, and when you pack

8
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:25,940
a wound instead.

9
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:30,087
If this feels confusing, it's usually
because of what people learned years

10
00:00:30,137 --> 00:00:34,386
ago. Tourniquets used to be the last
resort, because people thought they

11
00:00:34,436 --> 00:00:37,538
cost the limb. Military
experience flipped that.

12
00:00:37,588 --> 00:00:42,834
If you put one on right, and it's on
for the ride to the hospital, the limb

13
00:00:42,884 --> 00:00:44,754
is almost always fine.

14
00:00:44,804 --> 00:00:48,247
And waiting? That's
what gets people killed.

15
00:00:48,297 --> 00:00:53,207
So today, a life threatening bleed
from an arm or a leg gets a tourniquet

16
00:00:53,257 --> 00:00:54,057
right away.

17
00:00:57,014 --> 00:01:01,337
Before you treat it, look at it,
because the blood tells you which vessel

18
00:01:01,387 --> 00:01:04,212
is open. Arterial blood is bright red.

19
00:01:04,262 --> 00:01:07,844
That's the oxygen. And it spurts with
every heartbeat, because the heart

20
00:01:07,894 --> 00:01:09,417
is pushing it right out.

21
00:01:09,467 --> 00:01:12,934
It's under high pressure, so this is
the one that can kill fast, and it's

22
00:01:12,984 --> 00:01:14,174
the toughest to stop.

23
00:01:14,674 --> 00:01:19,691
Venous blood is darker red, and it flows
steadily instead of spurting, because

24
00:01:19,741 --> 00:01:21,851
there's not much pressure behind it.

25
00:01:21,901 --> 00:01:24,744
It can still be heavy
if a big vein is open.

26
00:01:24,794 --> 00:01:29,384
And capillary bleeding is a slow,
dark red ooze from the surface, like a

27
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:31,994
scraped knee. It usually stops on its own.

28
00:01:32,494 --> 00:01:34,481
Now, how much blood are we talking about?

29
00:01:34,531 --> 00:01:37,041
An adult has about five liters.

30
00:01:37,091 --> 00:01:40,924
If an adult suddenly loses about one
liter, roughly a fifth of it, that's

31
00:01:40,974 --> 00:01:46,414
serious. A child has a lot less, so around
five hundred milliliters is serious.

32
00:01:46,464 --> 00:01:50,196
And for an infant, just a hundred
to two hundred milliliters.

33
00:01:50,246 --> 00:01:53,958
So the same puddle on the floor is
a very different problem with a baby

34
00:01:54,008 --> 00:01:55,264
than with a grown man.

35
00:01:55,764 --> 00:01:58,434
So what makes a bleed life threatening?

36
00:01:58,484 --> 00:02:02,647
It's spurting, or it's pooling on
the floor fast, or it soaks through a

37
00:02:02,697 --> 00:02:05,944
dressing in seconds, or
part of the limb is gone.

38
00:02:05,994 --> 00:02:08,444
Any of those, and you're
thinking tourniquet.

39
00:02:08,944 --> 00:02:14,087
Quick check. Dark red blood, flowing
steadily from a deep cut on the shin.

40
00:02:14,137 --> 00:02:16,790
No spurting. Which kind of vessel is open?

41
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:25,880
A vein. Dark and steady, that's a vein.

42
00:02:25,930 --> 00:02:29,090
If it were bright and spurting,
you'd be thinking artery.

43
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:35,030
So why does pushing on
a wound work at all?

44
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:37,994
Picture the torn vessel under the skin.

45
00:02:38,044 --> 00:02:42,463
When you press, you squeeze the tissue
around it, and the vessel gets pinched

46
00:02:42,513 --> 00:02:47,300
flatter. Less blood gets through the
hole, and it comes out with less force.

47
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,766
And that's what buys you time.

48
00:02:49,816 --> 00:02:52,750
Your body already knows
how to plug a hole.

49
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:57,190
Platelets, tiny cell pieces in your
blood that help it clot, stick to the

50
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:59,850
torn edge, and a clot builds across it.

51
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:03,563
But a clot can't hold while
blood is blasting through.

52
00:03:03,613 --> 00:03:07,250
Pressure slows the flow
enough for the clot to form.

53
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:09,440
And most of the time, that's enough.

54
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:11,682
There are three ways to do it.

55
00:03:11,732 --> 00:03:13,170
Just your gloved hand.

56
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:16,023
Your gloved hand holding a dressing down.

57
00:03:16,073 --> 00:03:20,263
Or a pressure dressing, which is a
bandage wrapped firm enough that it keeps

58
00:03:20,313 --> 00:03:22,140
pressing even after you let go.

59
00:03:25,190 --> 00:03:27,284
Step one. Press hard.

60
00:03:27,334 --> 00:03:32,220
Your palm for a big wound, your fingers
for a small one, and push toward

61
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:36,516
a bone underneath if there is one, so the
vessel gets squeezed against something

62
00:03:36,566 --> 00:03:40,276
firm. If it's spurting,
don't go looking for gauze.

63
00:03:40,326 --> 00:03:45,762
Your gloved hand goes on the wound
now, and on an arm or a leg, you keep

64
00:03:45,812 --> 00:03:47,870
pressing while the tourniquet comes out.

65
00:03:48,370 --> 00:03:54,552
Step two, hold it. For massive bleeding,
you keep that pressure on all the

66
00:03:54,602 --> 00:03:59,445
way to the hospital. For a smaller
bleed, hold it steady without peeking,

67
00:03:59,495 --> 00:04:00,842
until the bleeding stops.

68
00:04:01,342 --> 00:04:04,744
Step three is the one people get wrong.

69
00:04:04,794 --> 00:04:08,124
Don't start by building
a big stack of gauze.

70
00:04:08,174 --> 00:04:12,644
A tall stack just soaks up blood,
and it puts cushion between your hand

71
00:04:12,694 --> 00:04:15,974
and the wound, so you're
actually pressing less.

72
00:04:16,474 --> 00:04:22,296
Step four. Once the bleeding's under
control, turn it into a pressure dressing.

73
00:04:22,346 --> 00:04:26,850
Wrap the bandage firmly, so the
pressure stays on without your hands.

74
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:31,960
And step five. Don't peel
off a dressing once it's on.

75
00:04:32,010 --> 00:04:36,508
The clot is forming in that gauze, and
ripping it off tears the clot right

76
00:04:36,558 --> 00:04:41,500
back open. If blood soaks through,
leave it there and press harder.

77
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:43,350
That just means you need to do more.

78
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:49,413
So what's more? On an arm or a leg,
if pressure isn't stopping it, that's

79
00:04:49,463 --> 00:04:54,253
a tourniquet. And in the spots where
a tourniquet can't go, you pack the

80
00:04:54,303 --> 00:04:59,053
wound, with plain gauze or hemostatic
gauze, gauze that helps the blood

81
00:04:59,103 --> 00:05:02,663
clot. But a life threatening limb bleed
doesn't wait for pressure to fail.

82
00:05:05,713 --> 00:05:09,183
There's one bleed where instinct gets
it wrong, and you'll see it all the

83
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:13,971
time. The nosebleed. Most
people tip the head back.

84
00:05:14,021 --> 00:05:17,531
Don't. The blood just runs down the
throat, it gets swallowed, it irritates

85
00:05:17,581 --> 00:05:20,721
the stomach, and now your
bleeding patient is vomiting too.

86
00:05:21,221 --> 00:05:25,043
Do the opposite. Sit them
upright and lean them forward.

87
00:05:25,093 --> 00:05:29,843
Then pinch the soft, fleshy part
of the nose, below the bony bridge.

88
00:05:29,893 --> 00:05:34,184
Most nosebleeds start near the front,
in that soft part, and bone can't

89
00:05:34,234 --> 00:05:37,059
be squeezed. That's
really all there is to it.

90
00:05:37,109 --> 00:05:39,053
Lean forward, pinch.

91
00:05:42,103 --> 00:05:46,337
Back to the garage. His blood is bright
red and spurting, and it's pushing

92
00:05:46,387 --> 00:05:48,421
straight through the gauze under her hand.

93
00:05:48,471 --> 00:05:51,653
That's a life threatening bleed from
a limb, so he gets a tourniquet.

94
00:05:51,703 --> 00:05:52,703
Right now.

95
00:05:53,203 --> 00:05:55,473
Use a commercial windlass tourniquet.

96
00:05:55,523 --> 00:05:56,753
It's got three parts.

97
00:05:56,803 --> 00:05:59,033
A wide strap that goes around the limb.

98
00:05:59,083 --> 00:06:01,893
A rod, called the windlass,
that you twist to tighten it.

99
00:06:01,943 --> 00:06:04,843
And a clip that locks the
rod so it can't unwind.

100
00:06:05,343 --> 00:06:07,500
Why not a belt, or a shirt and a stick?

101
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:10,257
Those improvised ones
fail a lot more often.

102
00:06:10,307 --> 00:06:13,940
They're usually too narrow to press hard
enough on the artery, so they squeeze

103
00:06:13,990 --> 00:06:16,098
the veins shut, but not the artery.

104
00:06:16,148 --> 00:06:18,806
And that can make the bleeding worse.

105
00:06:18,856 --> 00:06:20,326
We'll get to why in a minute.

106
00:06:20,376 --> 00:06:23,243
So only make one if you
really have nothing else.

107
00:06:26,293 --> 00:06:27,343
So, where does it go?

108
00:06:27,393 --> 00:06:31,083
Two to three inches above the wound,
between the wound and the heart, on

109
00:06:31,133 --> 00:06:34,003
healthy skin. Never over a joint.

110
00:06:34,053 --> 00:06:38,043
At the elbow or the knee, bone and
tendon shield the artery, so the strap

111
00:06:38,093 --> 00:06:39,143
can't squeeze it shut.

112
00:06:39,193 --> 00:06:43,143
His cut is near the wrist, so it goes
on his forearm, a few inches toward

113
00:06:43,193 --> 00:06:43,993
the elbow.

114
00:06:44,273 --> 00:06:46,543
There are three times
you do it differently.

115
00:06:46,593 --> 00:06:51,730
An amputation, more than one wound on
the same limb, or a limb so mangled

116
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:53,658
you can't tell where
the blood's coming from.

117
00:06:53,708 --> 00:06:57,460
Then you go high and tight, as
high up on the limb as you can.

118
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:00,130
Now, how tight does it need to be?

119
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:04,702
You twist until the bleeding stops,
and the distal pulse is gone.

120
00:07:04,752 --> 00:07:08,170
That's the pulse below the
tourniquet, at the wrist or the foot.

121
00:07:08,220 --> 00:07:11,320
You need both, and the
inside of the arm shows why.

122
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:15,836
Blood comes into the arm through an
artery, pushed hard by the heart.

123
00:07:15,886 --> 00:07:18,716
It goes back out through
veins, at low pressure.

124
00:07:18,766 --> 00:07:21,923
So a loose strap squeezes
the veins shut first.

125
00:07:21,973 --> 00:07:24,316
But the artery is still pumping blood in.

126
00:07:24,366 --> 00:07:28,560
Blood gets in and can't get out, so
the limb fills up, and the wound bleeds

127
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:31,863
harder. Twist further,
and the artery closes too.

128
00:07:31,913 --> 00:07:36,673
Now nothing gets in. The bleeding
stops, and the pulse below is gone.

129
00:07:37,173 --> 00:07:37,937
And it's going to hurt.

130
00:07:37,957 --> 00:07:42,113
A lot, usually. Tell him
that before you start.

131
00:07:42,163 --> 00:07:44,173
But don't loosen it just because it hurts.

132
00:07:44,673 --> 00:07:48,969
Quick check. A spurting cut on the
lower leg, just below the knee.

133
00:07:49,019 --> 00:07:52,409
If you measure two to three inches
up, you end up right on the knee.

134
00:07:52,459 --> 00:07:54,046
So where does the tourniquet go?

135
00:08:00,546 --> 00:08:02,589
On the thigh, above the knee.

136
00:08:02,639 --> 00:08:04,922
Never on the joint itself.

137
00:08:04,972 --> 00:08:08,719
If your two to three inches lands
on a joint, just go above the joint.

138
00:08:11,769 --> 00:08:13,719
Once it's on, write down the time.

139
00:08:13,769 --> 00:08:16,871
On the tourniquet itself,
and in your report.

140
00:08:16,921 --> 00:08:19,179
Then say it out loud at handoff.

141
00:08:19,229 --> 00:08:23,426
The longer it's on, the bigger the
risk to the limb, so the hospital needs

142
00:08:23,476 --> 00:08:24,276
to know.

143
00:08:24,469 --> 00:08:26,599
Leave it where people can see it.

144
00:08:26,649 --> 00:08:28,972
Never cover it with a
blanket or a dressing.

145
00:08:29,022 --> 00:08:31,782
The next person shouldn't
have to hunt for it.

146
00:08:31,832 --> 00:08:34,459
And don't loosen it or
take it off in the field.

147
00:08:34,509 --> 00:08:36,762
Loosen it, and the bleeding starts again.

148
00:08:37,262 --> 00:08:41,719
If it's as tight as it'll go and he's
still bleeding, don't keep cranking.

149
00:08:41,769 --> 00:08:45,489
Put a second tourniquet on, right
above the first, closer to the heart.

150
00:08:48,539 --> 00:08:51,609
Some places, there's just nothing
to wrap a tourniquet around.

151
00:08:51,659 --> 00:08:53,476
Like the groin, or the armpit.

152
00:08:53,526 --> 00:08:57,619
Those are called junctional areas,
where a limb meets the body.

153
00:08:58,119 --> 00:09:03,896
So you pack it. Push gauze into the
wound, plain or hemostatic, all the

154
00:09:03,946 --> 00:09:07,876
way down to where the blood is coming
from, and keep feeding it in until

155
00:09:07,926 --> 00:09:09,786
the space is packed tight.

156
00:09:10,286 --> 00:09:12,540
Then press hard on top.

157
00:09:12,590 --> 00:09:16,076
With hemostatic gauze,
at least three minutes.

158
00:09:16,126 --> 00:09:20,609
It speeds up clotting, but only where
it's actually touching the bleeding,

159
00:09:20,659 --> 00:09:23,692
so it needs to be in there,
and it needs that time.

160
00:09:23,742 --> 00:09:28,300
With plain gauze, keep pressing until
the bleeding's controlled, or you

161
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:33,556
hand off. If you just lay gauze on
top, that's a dressing, not packing,

162
00:09:33,606 --> 00:09:35,686
and it won't stop a bleed like this.

163
00:09:36,186 --> 00:09:41,496
There are two places you never
pack: the chest and the belly.

164
00:09:41,546 --> 00:09:44,543
There's nothing solid to
pack against in there.

165
00:09:44,593 --> 00:09:48,995
The gauze can end up somewhere you
can't control, and you can't press on

166
00:09:49,045 --> 00:09:50,643
what's bleeding inside.

167
00:09:50,693 --> 00:09:53,403
The neck is different again.

168
00:09:53,453 --> 00:09:58,287
A torn vein in the neck can suck in
air, so you cover it with an occlusive

169
00:09:58,337 --> 00:10:02,910
dressing, one that seals air
out, and press on that one side.

170
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,013
Never wrap anything around the neck.

171
00:10:09,063 --> 00:10:09,937
Let's put it all together.

172
00:10:09,987 --> 00:10:14,853
If it's small or slow,
you press, hold, and wrap.

173
00:10:14,903 --> 00:10:18,613
If it soaks through, leave
the dressing and do more.

174
00:10:18,663 --> 00:10:24,666
If an arm or a leg is bleeding badly
enough to kill, tourniquet first, two

175
00:10:24,716 --> 00:10:29,693
to three inches above, never on a joint,
and tight until the bleeding stops

176
00:10:29,743 --> 00:10:31,753
and the pulse below is gone.

177
00:10:31,803 --> 00:10:35,493
Groin or armpit, you pack it and press.

178
00:10:35,543 --> 00:10:39,183
And a nosebleed, lean forward and pinch.

179
00:10:42,233 --> 00:10:46,683
Problem one. You've held pressure on a
forearm cut, and blood starts soaking

180
00:10:46,733 --> 00:10:50,483
through your gauze. Your partner reaches
to peel it off and put on a fresh

181
00:10:50,533 --> 00:10:52,113
one. What do you do?

182
00:10:58,613 --> 00:11:01,806
Stop them. Leave it on.

183
00:11:01,856 --> 00:11:04,272
Peeling it off tears
the clot that's forming.

184
00:11:04,322 --> 00:11:08,909
Press harder, and if it still won't
stop, that forearm gets a tourniquet.

185
00:11:09,409 --> 00:11:13,339
Problem two. You tightened
a tourniquet on a thigh.

186
00:11:13,389 --> 00:11:17,907
The bleeding slowed but didn't stop,
and the foot still has a pulse.

187
00:11:17,957 --> 00:11:18,989
What's wrong?

188
00:11:25,489 --> 00:11:27,052
It isn't tight enough.

189
00:11:27,102 --> 00:11:31,412
The veins are shut, but the artery
isn't, so blood keeps coming in.

190
00:11:31,462 --> 00:11:35,412
Keep twisting until the bleeding
stops and the pulse is gone.

191
00:11:35,462 --> 00:11:38,622
And if it won't go any tighter,
add a second one above it.

192
00:11:39,122 --> 00:11:43,882
Problem three. A stab wound in
the groin, and it's pouring.

193
00:11:43,932 --> 00:11:45,572
Tourniquet, or pack it?

194
00:11:51,072 --> 00:11:55,666
Pack it. The groin is junctional,
so there's no limb to wrap.

195
00:11:55,716 --> 00:12:00,654
Get the gauze in, all the way down to
where it's bleeding, pack it tight,

196
00:12:00,704 --> 00:12:05,026
and then press hard. A lot of people
grab the tourniquet here, because it's

197
00:12:05,076 --> 00:12:07,736
the big tool. But it has nowhere to go.

198
00:12:10,786 --> 00:12:11,758
So that's external bleeding.

199
00:12:11,808 --> 00:12:16,056
The notes and practice for this
topic are free on levlprep.com.

200
00:12:16,106 --> 00:12:19,036
Next video, the bleeding you can't see.

201
00:12:19,086 --> 00:12:24,016
Internal bleeding, and how to catch
shock before the blood pressure drops.

202
00:12:24,066 --> 00:12:24,986
See you there.
